From 2be2f0cd17b1f45f8695c10b7ac72e90127a3d16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:09:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: rebuild profiles 7 and 8.1 and map them to SDR

On an output that takes no HDR10, a Dolby Vision stream of profile 7 or 8.1
played from its base layer, the HDR10 version of the film; with the tone
mapping method of a reference display (the commit before) that base layer is
mapped by the bridge with the static HDR10 metadata of the stream. Where that
method is the one of the video, such a stream is now reconstructed instead (its
RPU and, with a full enhancement layer, its residual) and mapped to SDR with the
level 1 metadata of each picture, as profile 5 is (SdrConversionEligible(),
BaseLayerIsHdr10()). With Kodi's own methods it plays as before; a method
changed to one of them while it plays has its base layer tone mapped by Kodi
again (SdrConversionHolds()). The method is that of the video settings of the
player, which the decoder and the decoder factory now ask
(ConversionTargetOf(stream, toneMapMethod)).

Measured with the software bridge (zink on lavapipe) on 14 pictures of a FEL
test stream, against their reconstruction in double precision with and without
the residual (a FEL picture; a MEL or 8.1 one), on an SDR display (BT.709, a 2.4
power, 100 cd/m2), the master's light with HDR reference white at the display's
white where the display can show it, within the active area, Delta E ITP:
  - with the residual: the median lower in all 13 pictures with such pixels
    (1.5 against 8.8, 9.0 against 39.3); the median hue error lower, or higher
    by at most 0.2 degrees, in all 13 with a clear colour;
  - without it: the 99th percentile lower in all 13 (11.2 against 25.8, 17.2
    against 24.8), and that of the hue error in all 13 (11.5 against 38.4); the
    median lower in 7 and higher in 6, by at most 2.0.
What remains between the rebuilt picture and its master there is the dynamic
tone mapping target of libplacebo: with an average from level 1 it maps into a
peak above the display's and lowers the whole picture by the ratio (0.89 to
0.98 here, by its formula and as measured). With that divided out, the median
of the pixels up to 5 cd/m2 of the rebuilt picture without the residual is at
most 0.2 in 11 of the 13 pictures that have them, and 1.5 and 2.7 in the other
two, where that of the base layer is 20.8 and 9.7. The base layer's metadata has
no average and gets no such ratio.

Fail closed, as the conversion is: a picture that cannot be reconstructed and
that the one before it cannot stand in for (after a seek, until the enhancement
layer starts again) is shown from its base layer, mapped by the bridge as such a
picture is when it is not reconstructed (ConversionShowsBase() with baseMapped,
RenderPlainMapping()); where that cannot be drawn either, by Kodi's own way.
After two seconds of film that could not be reconstructed, or where the
reconstruction never starts, the video plays as before. Not 8.4, 8.2 or 10.1:
not measured. A method changed to that of a reference display while a video
plays that opened with another reaches the reconstruction when its stream is
opened again; its base layer is mapped by the bridge until then.

The rule is tested on the host in every combination of its conditions (the rule
before does not build against the test; six mutations of it are caught); the
files it touches pass a syntax-only compile. Not run in Kodi.
